WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:05.749
She’s known for her dramatic screaming and
crying, and her seemingly dark power to predict

00:00:05.749 --> 00:00:10.470
death, the Banshee is seen as a terrifying
female spirit in Irish mythology.

00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:13.440
But is she really all that terrifying?

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:21.509
Although some of today’s pop culture has
weaponized the Banshee’s screams and made

00:00:21.509 --> 00:00:34.640
them an object of fear, the 
first banshees weren’t evil or even dangerous.

00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:38.440
She not only predicts your death, but helps
you prepare for it.

00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:43.000
This is one monster you might actually want
on your side.

00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:56.210
Going back to at least the 8thcentury, Irish
folk tradition depicts the Banshee as the

00:00:56.210 --> 00:00:59.460
spirit or ghost of a once living woman.

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:04.420
Banshees appear in two different forms: as
a withered, tiny crone with white hair; or

00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:07.180
as a tall, thin, beautiful woman.

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:12.850
The typical banshee has long, flowing hair
and appears dressed in white.

00:01:12.850 --> 00:01:18.080
Many times the woman’s spirit becomes a
banshee because of some injustice in her life,

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:22.670
because of bad or “improper” things she
did as a living woman, or was a victim of

00:01:22.670 --> 00:01:24.030
violence.

00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:28.850
Banshees only appear alone and don’t do
much of anything besides warn the living when

00:01:28.850 --> 00:01:30.210
death is coming.

00:01:30.210 --> 00:01:34.880
They forewarn the living of death by crying,
screaming, and clapping.

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:40.200
The Old Irish word “Bean sí” directly
translates to “fairy woman,” but etymologists

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:45.700
and folklorists agree that “sí” originally
meant ‘Otherworld’ so the name actually

00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:48.030
means ‘Woman of the Otherworld.”

00:01:48.030 --> 00:01:53.610
What’s especially unique about the banshee
is that they are only devoted to certain people;

00:01:53.610 --> 00:01:55.880
the ancient Gaelic nobility.

00:01:55.880 --> 00:02:00.640
Most often, people who have a surname that
begins with an “O,” “Mc,” or “Mac”

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:05.549
. A different banshee belonged to each noble
family and would forewarn deaths, sometimes

00:02:05.549 --> 00:02:07.400
appearing to the families’ servants.

00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:10.369
She is said to be an ancestor of the family
herself.

00:02:10.369 --> 00:02:15.280
In many ways, the banshee is a protector of
the family and its noble land, even a symbol

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:16.570
of the family.

00:02:16.570 --> 00:02:21.870
If any part of the ancestral home is still
standing, even if it’s a ruin, she will

00:02:21.870 --> 00:02:22.870
appear.

00:02:22.870 --> 00:02:27.459
Side note: Although my mom’s ancestors came
from the Munster province in Ireland, which

00:02:27.459 --> 00:02:33.569
is full of banshee myths, her family name
is Welsh, so unfortunately, no banshees for

00:02:33.569 --> 00:02:34.620
me.

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:39.530
Banshee legends became less common in the
16th and 17thcenturies—possibly because

00:02:39.530 --> 00:02:42.420
this is when the British government confiscated
Irish land.

00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:47.090
The English families who moved to Ireland
couldn’t have a banshee since they did not

00:02:47.090 --> 00:02:51.400
have Irish ancestry—even if they did seize
a noble title.

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:55.340
You can take my title, but you can never take
my banshee.

00:02:55.340 --> 00:03:00.340
Many old banshee stories are even classified
by the name of the family they are loyal to.

00:03:00.340 --> 00:03:05.120
A famous author, Thomas Crofton Croker, includes
“The Bunworth Banshee” and “The Mac

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:08.379
Carthy Banshee” in his book of Irish folklore.

00:03:08.379 --> 00:03:14.209
In both stories, the male heir’s death is
preceded by the wailing and clapping of a

00:03:14.209 --> 00:03:15.209
banshee.

00:03:15.209 --> 00:03:18.950
So, the banshee’s appearance warns of impending
death.

00:03:18.950 --> 00:03:21.319
But why the screaming and moaning?

00:03:21.319 --> 00:03:26.019
These expressions of mourning come from Irish
religious and cultural history.

00:03:26.019 --> 00:03:30.620
Women have played a crucial role in the Irish
culture of death from the very beginning.

00:03:30.620 --> 00:03:36.459
In their mythology, the ancient Celtic battle
goddess Catabodva is also known as Badb.

00:03:36.459 --> 00:03:42.870
A variant of her name, just happens to be
the south-eastern Irish name for banshee.

00:03:42.870 --> 00:03:48.209
Badb was said to not only influence battles,
but predict the deaths of notable warriors—by

00:03:48.209 --> 00:03:51.050
a loud, wailing cry.

00:03:51.050 --> 00:03:56.220
She could also shapeshift into a crow, an
ability some banshees have as well.

00:03:56.220 --> 00:04:00.830
Another goddess, Brigit, was said to be the
first in all of Ireland to cry and lament

00:04:00.830 --> 00:04:07.330
a death, that of her son—yet another connection
between women, death, and crying…or rather

00:04:07.330 --> 00:04:08.330
keening.

00:04:08.330 --> 00:04:13.909
In the Irish tradition, keening is a rhythmic
wailing and mourning performed by women as

00:04:13.909 --> 00:04:16.329
part of the death ritual.

00:04:16.329 --> 00:04:20.750
Keening was seen as necessary for the dead
to pass safely into the next world.

00:04:20.750 --> 00:04:25.440
Keening women were respected professionals
and performers who guided mourners through

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:27.900
grief with crying and singing.

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:32.610
They were viewed as in-between worlds, the
link between the living world and the Otherworld.

00:04:32.610 --> 00:04:37.911
Oh, and did I mention that yet another name
for the banshee is bean chaointe—which in

00:04:37.911 --> 00:04:41.180
Gaelic literally means “keening woman.”

00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:45.780
Since their actions were seen as necessary,
they were allowed to ignore social customs

00:04:45.780 --> 00:04:47.250
while performing.

00:04:47.250 --> 00:04:52.490
Keening women might walk barefoot, traveling
not on the roads, but through untamed countryside.

00:04:52.490 --> 00:04:57.270
They would appear disheveled, in torn clothes,
and unpinned and uncombed hair.

00:04:57.270 --> 00:05:02.520
They were outsiders, and these physical attributes
only served to emphasize their otherworldly

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:04.020
responsibilities.

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:09.919
Despite the supernatural air surrounding keening
women, they served a very practical function.

00:05:09.919 --> 00:05:13.120
In times of grief they acted as a catharsis.

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:18.120
Since they were paid professionals, for a
time only wealthy members of society could

00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:21.710
afford one, including those old noble families.

00:05:21.710 --> 00:05:25.910
So it makes sense that the banshee, who are
in many ways the ghost form of the keening

00:05:25.910 --> 00:05:29.510
woman, would only be associated with the upper
class.

00:05:29.510 --> 00:05:32.210
Goddesses, keening women, and banshees.

00:05:32.210 --> 00:05:36.830
It’s pretty easy to see that women controlled
the voice of death in Ireland.

00:05:36.830 --> 00:05:42.330
But I also have another theory, one that I
think explains the accounts of banshees “shrieking.”

00:05:42.330 --> 00:05:43.330
Barn owls.

00:05:43.330 --> 00:05:44.330
Hear me out.

00:05:44.330 --> 00:05:48.680
Barn owls make a truly terrifying screaming
sound.

00:05:48.680 --> 00:05:55.270
While they are currently endangered in Ireland,
they used to be widely spread across the country.

00:05:55.270 --> 00:05:58.990
Barn owls are nocturnal, hunting (and screeching)
at night.

00:05:58.990 --> 00:06:02.520
In the stories that I read, banshees only
appear at night.

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:07.040
The owls horrifying screeching could have
made its way into the Banshee myth.

00:06:07.040 --> 00:06:10.620
Maybe someone heard a barn owl’s cry in
the darkness the night before a death in the

00:06:10.620 --> 00:06:15.539
family and connected it to the keening Banshee
that also marks death.

00:06:15.539 --> 00:06:21.350
The banshee legend not only links Irish people
to their ancestors but emphasizes their belief

00:06:21.350 --> 00:06:22.850
in the supernatural.

00:06:22.850 --> 00:06:27.980
The Otherworld is important to the creation
of their literary and oral histories.

00:06:27.980 --> 00:06:32.770
With elements of mythical ancient goddesses
and real-life keening women, she connects

00:06:32.770 --> 00:06:57.330
other worlds as well: fact

00:06:57.330 --> 00:07:03.249
and fiction.

